NAME
    srv - manage the portable local lifecycle of a service

SYNOPSIS
    srv start <service>
    srv stop [-f] <service>
    srv host user install <service>
    srv host user uninstall <service>
    srv host user start <service>
    srv host user stop <service>
    srv host user restart <service>

DESCRIPTION
    srv provides the portable local service lifecycle owned by the technical m
    layer. The service process remains a foreground program from its own point of
    view; srv performs the surrounding background lifecycle management.

    For a provider-backed service, <service> is a facility identity. srv resolves
    the configured system facility default to one installed concrete provider,
    validates that concrete's materialized facility-service start realization, and
    launches the exact package command belonging to that concrete through the active
    m bootstrap. This preserves m command dispatch and normal package-launcher
    semantics independently of the host nohup PATH. Consumer package bindings are
    not consulted. The normal package launcher remains responsible for package HOME,
    environment, and dependency preparation.

    During migration, when no system facility default applies (or a legacy service
    name is outside facility-name grammar), srv retains the historical
    <service>-start PATH lookup as a compatibility path. A configured facility
    default that cannot resolve, or whose selected concrete has no valid service
    realization, fails instead of falling back to PATH.

COMMANDS
    start <service>
        Start the service if it is not already running under valid managed runtime
        state. Standard output and standard error from the service are appended to
        its managed service log.

    stop [-f] <service>
        Stop a managed running service with SIGTERM and wait a finite period for
        clean termination. If no active managed service remains, stop succeeds
        idempotently.

    host user install <service>
        Persist the service in the calling POSIX account's native user supervisor
        without starting it in the current session. The service must currently
        resolve through a valid system facility default/provider realization.
        Host-account discovery follows the actual POSIX account rather than
        caller-overridden HOME/XDG state roots.

    host user uninstall <service>
        Stop/unload the hosted service when necessary and remove its persistent
        native registration.

    host user start <service>
        Start the installed native user-supervisor registration. Current facility
        default intent is resolved for this launch.

    host user stop <service>
        Stop/unload the current hosted process while leaving the native registration
        installed.

    host user restart <service>
        Restart the installed hosted service through the native supervisor, resolving
        current facility default intent for the replacement process.

OPTIONS
    -f
        Apply only to stop. Bypass the normal caller-ownership check and explicitly
        request stopping the managed service. This is a lifecycle override, not an
        authentication or security boundary.

STATE
    srv resolves its user-scoped technical run and log roots through state-path:

        state-path user sys srv run
        state-path user sys srv log

    Runtime metadata below those roots is private to srv. Provider-backed running
    instances persist the selected concrete provider identity together with PID,
    caller-owner and exact command identity. Later facility-default changes do not
    retarget the running instance; stop operates on recorded runtime state and does
    not resolve another provider.

    Legacy runtime records without provider metadata remain readable during the
    migration compatibility period.

HOST USER SUPERVISION
    On Linux, srv resolves the calling account's UID and home from host account
    data, connects to that account's systemd user manager through /run/user/<uid>,
    and persists a user unit below the account home at .config/systemd/user. The
    unit uses /bin/sh as the fixed
    ExecStart executable and carries the exact m bootstrap, srv command, internal
    foreground runner and service identity as separately serialized arguments. This
    avoids systemd executable-path restrictions for relocatable RumiAI roots
    containing spaces or metacharacters.

    On macOS, srv resolves the calling account's UID and home from host account
    data, uses that account's launchd gui/<uid> domain and persists a LaunchAgent
    below the account home at Library/LaunchAgents. ProgramArguments is constructed
    with
    plutil so the exact argv values remain data rather than interpolated XML/shell
    source.

    The host supervisor manages the provider foreground process directly. It never
    invokes portable srv start. The persistent registration does not freeze a
    provider concrete: each host start/restart resolves the current system facility
    default and provider service realization. User host integration does not enable
    systemd linger or an automatic restart policy.

    srv host system is not part of the implemented surface.

READINESS
    Successful start establishes that the launched process survived the initial
    launch check. It does not establish application-specific readiness or health.

EXIT STATUS
    0     The requested lifecycle operation completed successfully. This includes
          idempotent stop cases where the service is not running.
    1     A lifecycle operation failed.
    2     The invocation, host scope/action, or service name is invalid.
    126   The legacy compatibility <service>-start target is not executable.
    127   No provider-backed default applies and the legacy compatibility
          <service>-start target cannot be resolved.

EXAMPLES
    Start a service named web:

        srv start web

    Stop it normally:

        srv stop web

    Explicitly stop it despite caller-ownership metadata:

        srv stop -f web

    Install and start it under the calling account's native host supervisor:

        srv host user install web
        srv host user start web

